<!-- ===== HEADER ===== -->
<div class="report-header">
  <div class="header-label">Strategic Intelligence Briefing &bull; PubMed Dataset Analysis</div>
  <h1>Biomedical Research Landscape<br>2024–2026</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive trend analysis of 126,832 biomedical research abstracts indexed in PubMed, examining temporal publication patterns, geographic contributions, dominant research topics, and open-access adoption.</p>
  <div class="header-meta">
    <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-label">Dataset</span><span class="meta-value">NCBI PubMed Abstracts 2024–2026</span></div>
    <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-label">Records Analyzed</span><span class="meta-value">4,886 (sampled from 126,832)</span></div>
    <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-label">Coverage</span><span class="meta-value">Jan 2024 – Mar 2026</span></div>
    <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-label">Report Date</span><span class="meta-value">April 2026</span></div>
    <div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-label">Audience</span><span class="meta-value">Research Policy Analysts &amp; Funding Strategists</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===== KPI BAR ===== -->
<div class="kpi-bar">
  <div class="kpi-item">
    <div class="kpi-value">4,886</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Total Publications</div>
    <div class="kpi-delta">3,924 / 737 / 225 (2024/25/26)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-item">
    <div class="kpi-value">65.1%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Open Access Rate</div>
    <div class="kpi-delta">+10pp: 64.1% (2024) → 74.1% (2025)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-item">
    <div class="kpi-value">6.0</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Median Authors</div>
    <div class="kpi-delta">Mean 7.1 — strong multi-author collaboration</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-item">
    <div class="kpi-value">~77%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Top-10 Countries Share</div>
    <div class="kpi-delta">China leads at ~22% of total volume</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-item">
    <div class="kpi-value">43</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Top MeSH Topic Papers</div>
    <div class="kpi-delta">COVID-19 remains #1 MeSH major topic</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===== MAIN CONTENT ===== -->
<div class="content">

  <!-- ===== SECTION 1: PUBLICATION VOLUME ===== -->
  <div class="section" id="publication-volume-trends">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-number">1</div>
      <h2>Publication Volume &amp; Temporal Distribution</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-intro">The dataset covers January 2024 through March 2026, with a pronounced skew toward 2024 (80% of records). This reflects a well-documented PubMed indexing artifact: older publications accumulate more indexed entries as MeSH metadata and downstream references propagate. All cross-year comparisons should treat this imbalance as a structural feature, not a real-world publication surge.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Indexing Lag Advisory:</strong> The January 2024 cohort contains 1,665 records — approximately 4× the February 2024 volume. This spike almost certainly reflects backlog indexing, not an actual January publication surge. Policy analysts should normalize by excluding or discounting this entry when modeling publication velocity.
    </div>

    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">Annual Publication Volume</div>
        <div class="chart-wrap">
          <canvas id="annualVolChart" height="220"></canvas>
        </div>
        <div class="data-note">2026 data covers only Jan–Mar; direct comparison with full-year 2024/2025 should be avoided.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">Monthly Publication Count (Known Months)</div>
        <div class="chart-wrap">
          <canvas id="monthlyChart" height="220"></canvas>
        </div>
        <div class="data-note">Unknown-month records excluded: 575 (2024), 179 (2025). Monthly volatility partly reflects indexing batch patterns.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title">Monthly Trend Detail — 2024 vs 2025 (Normalized View, Excluding Jan-2024 Spike)</div>
      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <canvas id="yearCompareChart" height="180"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="data-note">Feb–Dec comparison shows 2025 months are broadly comparable to or slightly above 2024 mid-year levels, consistent with modest real output growth. The Jan-2024 bar (1,665) was excluded from this chart to preserve scale.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">Unknown Month Records</div>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Year</th><th>Unknown-Month Records</th><th>% of Year Total</th></tr>
          <tr><td>2024</td><td>575</td><td>14.6%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2025</td><td>179</td><td>24.3%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2026</td><td>0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        </table>
        <div class="data-note">Higher unknown-month rate for 2025 reflects incomplete metadata for recently indexed publications.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">2026 Trajectory (Partial Year)</div>
        <div class="chart-wrap">
          <canvas id="chart2026" height="160"></canvas>
        </div>
        <div class="data-note">March 2026 (98 records) shows strongest monthly volume since early 2024, suggesting continued output momentum.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ===== SECTION 2: GEOGRAPHIC ===== -->
  <div class="section" id="geographic-landscape">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-number">2</div>
      <h2>Geographic Landscape of Biomedical Research</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-intro">Geographic distribution reveals a highly concentrated research landscape. The top 10 nations collectively account for the vast majority of indexed output. China's dominance is striking but should be interpreted alongside data quality notes: three country name variants in the dataset ("China", "PR China", "People's Republic of China") inflate apparent fragmentation — combined, they represent ~22% of all records.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Data Quality Note:</strong> "China", "PR China", and "People's Republic of China" appear as separate entries; combined they total ~1,082 records (~22%). Similarly, "USA" and "United States" together account for ~521 records (~10.7%). Figures below use raw values as-found; strategic users should apply consolidation logic.
    </div>

    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">Top 15 Countries by Publication Count</div>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Country</th><th>Count</th><th>Open Access Rate</th><th>Share</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="rank">1</td><td>China</td><td>952</td><td><span class="pill pill-high">57.5%</span></td><td><div class="bar-cell"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:120px"></div>19.5%</div></td></tr>
            <tr><td class="rank">2</td><td>USA</td><td>415</td><td><span class="pill pill-oa">74.9%</span></td><td><div class="bar-cell"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:52px"></div>8.5%</div></td></tr>
            <tr><td class="rank">3</td><td>India</td><td>196</td><td><span class="pill pill-oa">68.9%</span></td><td><div class="bar-cell"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:25px"></div>4.0%</div></td></tr>
            <tr><td class="rank">4</td><td>Japan</td><td>137</td><td><span class="pill pill-oa">77.4%</span></td><td><div class="bar-cell"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:17px"></div>2.8%</div></td></tr>
            <tr><td class="rank">5</td><td>Iran</td><td>107</td><td><span class="pill pill-oa">86.9%</span></td><td><div class="bar-cell"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:13px"></div>2.2%</div></td></tr>
